// @run
// @log 2
// @log 1
// @log 2
// @log 7
// @hint redundant type annotation: `a` is inferred as `({r: i32} | null)[]` — remove it to lean on inference
// THE HINT IS DECLARED, NOT REMOVED: `const a: (Circle | null)[]` is the destination that
// PINS the literal's element row to the niche, which is the whole subject of this fixture.
// Deleting the annotation to silence the hint deletes the defect.
// THE NICHE SOURCE ROW (D811). One un-annotated list literal with TWO declared
// destinations: a `(Circle | null)[]` — a NICHE, kind 1 with the `| null` bit, backed by
// `(array (mut (ref null $Circle)))` where the null is a legal ELEMENT VALUE — and a union
// BOX list. D791 built the element-converting copy for this shape and D792 declined this
// one element pair, because boxing a niche element under the ARM's tag makes a box whose
// tag says `Circle` and whose payload is null, and the narrow the box exists for compiles a
// `ref.cast` that TRAPS on it.
//
// WHAT A NULL BECOMES IS THE DESTINATION UNION'S OWN REP FOR ONE: a box carrying
// `nullBoxTag()` with a `ref.null none` payload, which is exactly what `(Shape | null)[]`
// builds for a `null` literal element. So the copy's loop body tests `ref.is_null` per
// element and picks between two tags — not a second lowering, eight lines inside the
// existing one.
//
// THIS FIXTURE IS THE ONE THAT CAN SEE IT. The eight corpus cells this closes are all
// `[{ r: 7 }]` with no null anywhere, so a copy that wrote the arm's tag over a null grades
// identically on every one of them. Here `b[1]` IS null and `b[0]` is not: ablate the
// two-tag loop body and the `== null` reads FALSE for both (check-clean, silently wrong),
// and narrowing `b[1]` to `Circle` traps with `wasm trap: cast failure`.
type Circle = { r: i32 }
type Sq = { s: i32 }
type Shape = Circle | Sq
function f() {
  const xs = [{ r: 7 }, null]
  const a: (Circle | null)[] = xs
  const b: (Shape | null)[] = xs
  print(a.length)
  const g1 = b[1]
  if g1 == null { print(1) } else { print(2) }
  const g0 = b[0]
  if g0 == null { print(1) } else { print(2) }
  if g0 != null {
    if g0 is Circle { print(g0.r) }
  }
}
f()
